      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the total number of blue and green tiles in the original figure and then compare them after the new border is added.\n\nFirst, let's visualize the original figure. The figure appears to be a complex arrangement of blue and green hexagons, but we can deduce some critical patterns. Consider the shape formed by the largest hexahedron and the surrounding smaller ones that form a frame.\n\nWe can see that the new configuration will have additional green tiles formed by extending the existing green tiles. To find the pattern, let's look at how the additional tiles are attached.\n\nThe border is a continuous line of green tiles that extends around the entire structure. Considering the symmetry of the hexagonal shapes, each vertex of the hexagons will contribute to the new border, forming a curved line that wraps around the original figure.\n\nThe key observation is that each vertex (or intersection) of the blue tiles will be surrounded by new green tiles. Since the original configuration was a figure composed entirely of blue and green hexagons, and adding a border will simply extend the green tiles, we can infer that each vertex of the more complex structure will contribute one additional tile.\n\nIn a complex figure made up of blue and green hexagons, each vertex (blue!) forms a quadrilateral around it, meaning there are four angles, turning in a 435-degree manner and then 45-degree turning on each edge extending from the hexahedron. This means that each vertex of the hexahedron and the surrounding structure will collectively create a symmetrical new border pattern.\n\nTherefore, the pattern of the new border is much interconnected. The sum of all the contributions of the figure will be a symmetric arrangement of four sides (due to the symmetrical nature) each making the difference:\n\n4 *(435/360) + 15 *(435/360) + 6 *(435/360) + 6 *(45/360) = 47.45\n\nHowever, considering the traversal of the single unit hexahed